<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jungle Commando</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1986">1986</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Surinamese Liberation Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jungle Commando was a Maroon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronnie-brunswijk">Ronnie Brunswijk</a> that fought the Surinamese National Army in the interior war of 1986 to 1992 against the military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desi-bouterse">Desi Bouterse</a>. The fighting devastated Maroon villages in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, including Moiwana, and sent thousands of refugees into French Guiana. A peace accord in 1992 ended the conflict.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The group's formal name was the Suriname National Liberation Army; locals called it the Jungle Commando. Ronnie Brunswijk, a former soldier who had taken part in the military coup of 1980, founded it in 1986 to secure equal rights for Suriname's Maroon minority and to bring down the military leadership. On 22 July 1986 he and a few other deserters attacked 12 soldiers at an outpost at Stolkertsijver and held them hostage while another attack struck the army barracks at Albina. The army destroyed the temple at Moengotapoe and seized all the men there, and in Moiwana, Brunswijk's birth village, at least 35 people were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The fighting centred on the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moengo">Moengo</a>. On 20 August 1986 the Suriname Aluminum Company shut its bauxite plants there, and the next day the Jungle Commando attacked an army platoon at Ajumara Kondre. It came to control the Marowijne, Lawa and Tapanahoni rivers and hijacked boats and aircraft. After it blew up two bridges on the east–west highway in November, the government declared a state of emergency on 1 December, and the army drove it from Moengo the next day. In January 1987 its attacks on power lines cut the Afobaka dam's supply and blacked out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>.</p><p>A new constitution was approved in a referendum in September 1987 and elections followed on 25 November, but the army went on executing locals it called rebels. In 1989 the Jungle Commando burned the village of Pokigron, struck Brownsweg and on 31 August hijacked a ferry on the Coppename River; in October it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moengo">Moengo</a> again with a splinter group led by Kofi Ajomopong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A ceasefire agreed in June 1989 was formally recognised in an accord on 21 July, but the army rejected it, and Amerindian leaders opposed the plan to take the former fighters into the police. Talks resumed at the presidential palace on 24 March 1990 and broke down amid reports of drug trafficking, after more than 100 kilos of cocaine were found on an aircraft near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moengo">Moengo</a>. After another military coup, the newly elected president set up a Peace Commission in 1992, which began the process that ended the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jungle Commando” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After independence the small Surinamese National Army became a political force: sergeants seized power in 1980, and from 1986 to 1992 the army fought the <b>Jungle Commando</b> of Ronnie Brunswijk in the interior war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Military</a></li></ul></section>
